{"ok":true,"key":"rd_spots","name":"Spots","method":"Reaction–Diffusion","exhibit":"Turing Patterns","phenomenon":"Pattern formation","question":"Which parameter changes alter the morphology?","status":"numerical","status_label":"Numerical check","blurb":"Two chemicals reacting and diffusing at different rates (the Gray–Scott system) settle from noise into a field of spots. The resemblance to animal markings is suggestive; it does not establish a biological mechanism.","physics":"In 1952 Alan Turing proposed that the patterns of nature — a leopard's spots, a zebra's stripes, the whorls of a seashell — could arise spontaneously from two chemicals that react and diffuse, with no blueprint guiding them. The trick is differential diffusion: a slowly-spreading 'activator' locally amplifies itself while a fast-spreading 'inhibitor' suppresses it nearby. Local growth plus long-range suppression carves a regular length scale out of a uniform soup.\n\nThe Gray–Scott model is the classic two-chemical realisation. U is fed in steadily; the reaction U + 2V → 3V converts it to V (V autocatalyses); V slowly decays. Tiny initial noise grows into structure, and the precise balance between the feed rate F and the kill rate k decides which structure: isolated spots, labyrinthine stripes, or — astonishingly — blobs that grow, pinch in two and replicate like dividing cells.\n\nThe same activator–inhibitor mathematics models animal coat markings, the spacing of hair follicles and the ridges of fingerprints, vegetation patterns in arid landscapes, and chemical waves in the lab.","terms":"• U, V — the two reacting/diffusing chemical concentrations\n• Du, Dv — their diffusion rates; the pattern needs Du > Dv (activator V spreads slower)\n• U V² — the autocatalytic reaction that converts U into V\n• F(1−U) — the steady feed replenishing U\n• (F+k)V — feed plus kill removing V\n• ∇² — the Laplacian (diffusion); a 9-point isotropic stencil here","numerics":"Explicit time stepping of the two coupled reaction–diffusion equations on a periodic grid, with a 9-point isotropic Laplacian for the diffusion term. The (F, k) pair is taken from Pearson's classification to select each regime.","ic":"A uniform field (u = 1, v = 0) with a few small seeded patches of v to nucleate the pattern.","bc":"Periodic in both directions; the feed and kill rates select the regime (spots, stripes, labyrinth or mitosis).","validation":"Numerical check: the (F, k) values reproduce the morphologies of Pearson's classification; the explicit scheme clips concentrations to [0, 1] each step (an intervention, reported).","eq":"data:image/png;base64,","clip":"https://github.com/SalehMohammadrezaei/funoos-web/releases/download/media-v1/gray_scott__rd_spots__pattern.mp4","poster":"media/posters/gray_scott__rd_spots__pattern.webp","preset":{"pattern":"Spots"},"layers":[{"id":"question","title":"The question","text":"Which parameter changes alter the morphology?"},{"id":"see","title":"What the colours and motion show","text":"Two chemicals reacting and diffusing at different rates (the Gray–Scott system) settle from noise into a field of spots. The resemblance to animal markings is suggestive; it does not establish a biological mechanism."},{"id":"try","title":"One change to try","items":["Open the Custom Explorer scene and move F and k in small steps."]},{"id":"observe","title":"What to measure","text":"The pattern coverage saturating as the domain fills.","fixed":[],"more":["Change the seed (advanced)."]},{"id":"physics","title":"Physical explanation","text":"In 1952 Alan Turing proposed that the patterns of nature — a leopard's spots, a zebra's stripes, the whorls of a seashell — could arise spontaneously from two chemicals that react and diffuse, with no blueprint guiding them. The trick is differential diffusion: a slowly-spreading 'activator' locally amplifies itself while a fast-spreading 'inhibitor' suppresses it nearby. Local growth plus long-range suppression carves a regular length scale out of a uniform soup.\n\nThe Gray–Scott model is the classic two-chemical realisation. U is fed in steadily; the reaction U + 2V → 3V converts it to V (V autocatalyses); V slowly decays. Tiny initial noise grows into structure, and the precise balance between the feed rate F and the kill rate k decides which structure: isolated spots, labyrinthine stripes, or — astonishingly — blobs that grow, pinch in two and replicate like dividing cells.\n\nThe same activator–inhibitor mathematics models animal coat markings, the spacing of hair follicles and the ridges of fingerprints, vegetation patterns in arid landscapes, and chemical waves in the lab."},{"id":"model","title":"Equations, symbols and assumptions","eq":"$\\partial_t U = D_u\\nabla^2U - UV^2 + F(1-U),\\ \\ \\partial_t V = D_v\\nabla^2V + UV^2 - (F{+}k)V$","text":"• U, V — the two reacting/diffusing chemical concentrations\n• Du, Dv — their diffusion rates; the pattern needs Du > Dv (activator V spreads slower)\n• U V² — the autocatalytic reaction that converts U into V\n• F(1−U) — the steady feed replenishing U\n• (F+k)V — feed plus kill removing V\n• ∇² — the Laplacian (diffusion); a 9-point isotropic stencil here"},{"id":"setup","title":"Initial and boundary conditions (this preset)","ic":"A uniform field (u = 1, v = 0) with a few small seeded patches of v to nucleate the pattern.","bc":"Periodic in both directions; the feed and kill rates select the regime (spots, stripes, labyrinth or mitosis).","preset":{"pattern":"Spots"}},{"id":"numerics","title":"Numerical method, checks and limitations","text":"Explicit time stepping of the two coupled reaction–diffusion equations on a periodic grid, with a 9-point isotropic Laplacian for the diffusion term. The (F, k) pair is taken from Pearson's classification to select each regime.","checks":"Numerical check: the (F, k) values reproduce the morphologies of Pearson's classification; the explicit scheme clips concentrations to [0, 1] each step (an intervention, reported).","status":"Numerical check"},{"id":"refs","title":"References","items":["Pearson, J.E. (1993). Complex patterns in a simple system. Science 261, 189–192.","Turing, A.M. (1952). The chemical basis of morphogenesis. Phil. Trans. R. Soc. B 237, 37–72.","Kondo, S. & Miura, T. (2010). Reaction–diffusion model as a framework for understanding biological pattern formation. Science 329, 1616–1620."]}],"experiment":{"id":"gray_scott","name":"Gray–Scott patterns","question":"Which parameter changes alter the morphology?","preset_label":"Spots","presets":[{"key":"rd_spots","label":"Spots","exhibit":"Turing Patterns"},{"key":"rd_stripes","label":"Stripes/coral","exhibit":"Turing Patterns"},{"key":"rd_maze","label":"Labyrinth","exhibit":"Turing Patterns"},{"key":"rd_mitosis","label":"Replication","exhibit":"Turing Patterns"},{"key":"rd_custom","label":"Custom","exhibit":"Turing Patterns"}],"compare":[]},"cmap":null,"params":[{"name":"pattern","label":"Pattern","type":"choice","group":"Geometry","choices":["Spots","Stripes","Maze","Mitosis","Coral","Waves","Custom"],"default":"Spots","help":"Each pattern is a (feed F, removal k) regime of the Gray–Scott model from Pearson's classification; Custom lets you set F and k yourself."},{"name":"F","label":"Feed rate F","type":"float","default":0.035,"min":0.01,"max":0.08,"group":"Physics","help":"Rate at which U is fed in (and V removed with k).","hard_min":0,"hard_max":0.3,"when":["pattern",["Custom"]]},{"name":"k","label":"Removal rate k","type":"float","default":0.065,"min":0.04,"max":0.075,"group":"Physics","help":"Rate at which V is removed.","hard_min":0,"hard_max":0.3,"when":["pattern",["Custom"]]},{"name":"Du","label":"Diffusivity of U","type":"float","default":0.16,"min":0.05,"max":0.25,"group":"Physics","help":"Diffusion coefficient of U (explicit scheme: keep Du ≤ 0.25 for stability at dt = 1).","hard_min":0,"hard_max":0.25,"advanced":true},{"name":"Dv","label":"Diffusivity of V","type":"float","default":0.08,"min":0.02,"max":0.2,"group":"Physics","help":"Diffusion coefficient of V. Patterns need Dv < Du (Turing's condition).","hard_min":0,"hard_max":0.25,"advanced":true},{"name":"seed","label":"Random seed","type":"int","default":1,"min":0,"max":9999,"group":"Geometry","help":"Seed of the initial noisy blobs (same seed → same pattern).","hard_min":0,"hard_max":2147483647,"advanced":true},{"name":"resolution","label":"Resolution","type":"choice","choices":["Low (fast)","Medium","High","Ultra (slow)"],"default":"Medium","group":"Render","help":"Grid resolution. It changes the grid, and with it whatever a scene ties to the grid: the elapsed convective time in the wind tunnel, the Rayleigh number in the convection scenes, the grain size in the rock, the extent of the blast box. The derived readouts say what moved for this experiment; runs at different resolutions are comparable only where they say so."},{"name":"duration","label":"Duration (×)","type":"float","default":1.0,"min":0.2,"max":6.0,"hard_min":0.05,"hard_max":20.0,"group":"Render","help":"Simulated interval as a multiple of the scene's reference interval (1.0 = default)."}],"method_label":"Reaction–Diffusion · Gray–Scott","web_case":"gray_scott__rd_spots"}